WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:18.040
This video will teach you how to inject your
insulin using a syringe.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:23.080
Please watch the entire video before injecting
your insulin.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:27.520
Your healthcare provider may recommend that
you inject insulin using a syringe to help

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:30.739
you control your blood sugar level.

00:00:30.739 --> 00:00:36.110
Injecting insulin with a syringe involves
choosing an injection site, preparing the

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:42.300
syringe and insulin, and then injecting the
insulin into your body.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:47.930
Your healthcare professional will tell you
what type of insulin you need as well as when

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:51.960
and how much you need to inject.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:01:01.329
You will need alcohol wipes, your bottle of
insulin, a new syringe for each injection,

00:01:01.329 --> 00:01:06.830
and a sharps disposal container to throw away
the used syringes.

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:21.140
A disposable syringe has the following parts:
a barrel, a plunger, a needle cap, and a needle.

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:26.490
Selecting the Injection Site
Before injecting your insulin, select the

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:28.100
inection site on your body.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:35.530
The areas for insulin injection include the
abdomen or belly, upper arms, the thighs,

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:38.909
and the buttocks.

00:01:38.909 --> 00:01:44.460
When selecting the injection site, be sure
you use a different spot each time you give

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:47.140
yourself an injection.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:53.240
Leave at least one inch away from the last
injection spot, inject your insulin at least

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:59.049
two inches from your belly button, and choose
a spot at least one inch or farther from any

00:01:59.049 --> 00:02:03.229
scar or mole.

00:02:03.229 --> 00:02:08.640
Preparing the Syringe and Insulin
Step One: wash your hands with soap and water

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.250
and then dry them.

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:16.900
Step Two: If your insulin needs to be mixed,
gently roll the bottle between your hands.

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:24.590
Do not shake the bottle because it may cause
bubbles to form in your insulin.

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:29.260
Before injecting yourself, check the insulin
in the bottle to make sure it is the type

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:37.249
of insulin your doctor prescribed for you,
not past the expiration date, and the insulin

00:02:37.249 --> 00:02:41.029
is free of clumps.

00:02:41.029 --> 00:02:44.959
Step Three: remove the cap from your insulin
bottle.

00:02:44.959 --> 00:02:51.329
Use an alcohol wipe to clean the rubber stopper
on the top of the bottle.

00:02:51.329 --> 00:02:54.620
Step Four: Remove the syringe from its package.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:59.639
Be sure to use a new syringe each time you
inject insulin.

00:02:59.639 --> 00:03:04.419
Step Five: remove the needle cap from the
syringe.

00:03:04.419 --> 00:03:09.520
Do not touch the needle or allow it to touch
any surface, and do not use the syringe if

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:11.609
the needle is bent.

00:03:11.609 --> 00:03:18.659
Some syringes have a cap over the plunger
that you may need to remove as well.

00:03:18.659 --> 00:03:24.120
Step Six: pull back the plunger to draw in
an amount of air that is equal to your insulin

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:25.989
dose.

00:03:25.989 --> 00:03:31.519
Step Seven: Hold your insulin bottle securely
on a flat surface.

00:03:31.519 --> 00:03:35.109
Push the needle through the rubber on the
top of the bottle.

00:03:35.109 --> 00:03:40.079
Push the plunger to inject the air into the
insulin bottle.

00:03:40.079 --> 00:03:41.650
Leave the needle in the bottle.

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:46.269
This helps to keep the right amount of pressure
in the bottle and makes it easier to draw

00:03:46.269 --> 00:03:49.230
insulin into the syringe.

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:57.030
Step Eight: With the needle still in the bottle,
turn the bottle and syringe upside down.

00:03:57.030 --> 00:04:02.340
Step Nine: Pull back the plunger to fill the
syringe will just a little more than the insulin

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:04.370
dose you need.

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:08.479
If you see air bubbles, tap the barrel of
the syringe with your finger to make them

00:04:08.479 --> 00:04:10.370
rise to the top.

00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:17.850
Slowly, push in the plunger just enough to
push out the air and extra insulin.

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:23.250
Step Ten: Carefully check to make sure the
amount of insulin in the syringe matches your

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:26.080
insulin dose.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:32.490
Pull the needle out of the bottle and carefully
lay the syringe on a flat, clean surface.

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:36.980
Make sure the needle does not touch anything.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:41.950
Injecting the Insulin
Step One: Clean your injection site with an

00:04:41.950 --> 00:04:43.450
alcohol wipe.

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:47.900
Use a circular motion to clean a spot about
two inches wide.

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:53.280
Step Two: Pinch a two-inch fold of skin in
the clean injection site.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:58.340
Step Three: Hold the syringe like a pencil
and quickly insert the needle straight into

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:01.260
your skin at a 90-degree angle.

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:04.890
Make sure the whole needle enters your skin.

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:10.390
Step Four: Push the plunger all the way down
to inject the insulin into the fat tissue

00:05:10.390 --> 00:05:11.670
beneath your skin.

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:16.450
Slowly, count to five before removing the
needle to make sure you have injected all

00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:18.740
of the insulin.

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:24.460
Step Five: Pull the needle straight out to
remove it.

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:28.750
Step Six: Throw away the syringe into your
sharps disposal container.

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:35.220
Never throw your syringes or needles directly
into the trash.

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:39.870
Contact your healthcare provider if you have
questions about injecting your insulin or

00:05:39.870 --> 00:05:44.190
if your blood sugar level stays above or below
your target range.

